<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniel Salamanca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Daniel Salamanca Urey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> from 1931 to 1934. Amid the economic crisis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, he pursued a hard line over the disputed Chaco region and in 1932 led Bolivia into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. After a series of defeats and bitter quarrels with his commanders, he was arrested by army officers at Villamontes in November 1934 and forced to resign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniel-salamanca.jpg" alt="Daniel Salamanca" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Salamanca._Unknown_author._c._1931%E2%80%931934._La_Paz._Cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daniel Domingo Salamanca Urey was born in Cochabamba on 8 July 1869 into a wealthy landed family and studied law at the Higher University of San Simón. He married the poet Sara Ugarte de Salamanca, with whom he had five children. Elected to the Chamber of Deputies for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> in 1899, he was made Finance Minister by President José Manuel Pando two years later. In 1907, as a leading opposition figure, he attacked the Pinilla-Soler Treaty, which had divided the Chaco between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>.</p><p>He later left the Liberals, helped found the Republican Party and ran unsuccessfully for vice-president in 1917. Opposed to the ambitions of the Republican leader Bautista Saavedra, he founded the Genuine Republican Party with Juan Maria Escalier and others, and lost the presidential election of 1925 to Saavedra's chosen successor, Hernando Siles. He then withdrew to teach law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the military overthrow of Siles in 1930, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> led a coalition with the Liberals, with José Luis Tejada Sorzano as his running mate, and took office in March 1931. He introduced an unpopular austerity programme and clamped down on the opposition. A long-standing hawk on the Chaco, he took as his motto We must stand firm in the Chaco. The discovery of oil in the foothills of the Andes raised hopes of oil in the Chaco, and he ordered more small garrisons, fortines, to be set up. In 1932 a Bolivian unit attacked the Paraguayan post at the lake the Paraguayans called Pitiantutá, which in effect began the war.</p><p>Relations with the high command were poor from the start: he placed the German Hans Kundt at the head of the army, and after defeats on the southern front Kundt was replaced by General Enrique Peñaranda at the end of 1933. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> blamed his generals for the setbacks and decided to replace Peñaranda and several commanders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The generals, among them Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-toro">David Toro</a> and Major Germán Busch, put Tejada in his place; it has been alleged that Tejada was part of the plot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> retired to Cochabamba and died of stomach cancer on 17 July 1935, days after the ceasefire. Many blamed him for the war, while others saw him as betrayed by his officers. Bolivian historians call him El Hombre Símbolo, the symbolic man.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daniel Salamanca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cochabamba, in what is now <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Died in Cochabamba, in what is now <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.39,-66.16&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Cochabamba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Daniel Salamanca"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4498 3944h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4498 3944h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.39,-66.16&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
